        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Type of Caulk Is Best for Outdoor Use?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Elastomeric latex or a paintable polymer is best for anything that will be painted, and 100 percent silicone is best for unpainted joints that take a lot of water. Every exterior sealant on the shelf falls into one of a few chemical families, and the family predicts its behavior far better than the brand does. The front of the tube is advertising. The chemistry in the small print decides how far the bead can stretch, whether paint will bond to it and which surfaces it will hold. Learn the five families and choosing a tube takes about a minute.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Acrylic latex and elastomeric latex</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Acrylic latex</strong> is the least expensive family and the most forgiving to use. Water cleans it up, it tools smooth and it is ready for paint in an hour or two. Where it falls down is movement: most basic latex caulks are rated for joint movement in the low single-digit percentages, which suits interior trim and does a poor job on siding. <strong>Elastomeric latex</strong>, the family that includes Dynaflex 230 and Big Stretch, keeps the water cleanup but stretches several times further, and that is why it has become the default for painted exterior joints.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Silicone, polymers and hybrids</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Silicone</strong> resists water and UV better than any other family, and it is also the hardest to live with: paint will not stick to it and it holds poorly on porous wood and masonry. <strong>Polymer and hybrid sealants</strong>, a group that covers polyurethane, MS polymer, the OSI Quad line and tripolymers such as Geocel 2300, pair strong adhesion with paintability and good movement. Cleanup is harder and the tubes cost more, but these are what professionals reach for on vinyl, fiber cement, metal and masonry.
          </p>

