          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Butyl rubber, and the one rule to remember</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Butyl rubber</strong> rounds out the five, and for any joint people will see, most homeowners should pass on it. It never loses its tack, it picks up dirt and it takes a very long time to firm up, though it still earns a spot on gutter seams and metal roof laps. If you keep one rule from this section, keep this one: a joint that will be painted gets elastomeric latex or a paintable polymer, and a joint that will never be painted and takes heavy water gets silicone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Do You Read an Exterior Caulk Label?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Turn the tube over and find the ASTM standard first, because it tells you the movement rating, the single most useful number on any sealant. Two standards matter to homeowners. <strong>ASTM C834</strong> applies to latex sealants and sets a fairly low bar; a tube that claims only C834 is, for practical purposes, a painter's caulk. <strong>ASTM C920</strong> applies to elastomeric joint sealants and adds a class number stating how much movement the joint can take.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What do Class 25, 35 and 50 mean?</h3>

          <p>
            A C920 Class 25 sealant is built to handle movement of plus or minus 25 percent of the joint width, and Class 35 and Class 50 products handle more. Some labels add letters for grade, where NS stands for non-sag and means the bead stays put in a vertical joint, and for use, covering traffic, immersion or particular substrates. There is no need to learn every code. What matters is that a C920 class number is a claim that can be checked, which makes it worth more than any adjective printed on the front.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What the Movement Classes Mean</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">±25%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">ASTM C920 Class 25</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">±35%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">ASTM C920 Class 35</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">±50%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">ASTM C920 Class 50</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Low</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical ASTM C834 latex</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much does a real joint move?</h3>

          <p>
            Put that in terms of an actual gap. A 3/8-inch joint between two wood boards on a sunny wall can open and close by a sixteenth of an inch or more over a year. That works out to about 17 percent of its width: inside the range of a Class 25 sealant and beyond what a plain latex caulk can follow. Wider joints are more forgiving because the same movement is a smaller share of the width, which is part of the reason sealant makers advise against joints narrower than about a quarter inch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three more numbers to check before you buy</h3>

          <p>
            After the movement rating, look for three more figures on the label: the lowest application temperature, the wait before rain, and the wait before paint. Together they decide whether the job can happen this weekend, and they differ between products more than most buyers expect. When a tube is vague on any of the three, pull up the maker's technical data sheet, which nearly always spells them out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surface" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Caulk Works on Wood, Vinyl, Brick and Metal?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use elastomeric latex on painted wood, a polymer on vinyl and fiber cement, a polymer or tripolymer on masonry, and silicone on unpainted glass and metal. Adhesion is the second half of the choice. A sealant with excellent stretch does no good if it lets go of one side of the joint, and each family holds different materials. Name both sides of every joint before you buy, because the harder of the two surfaces sets the product.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wood siding and trim</h3>

          <p>
            Painted wood is the most forgiving surface. Elastomeric latex such as Dynaflex 230 or Big Stretch grips it, takes paint and keeps up with seasonal movement. Keep silicone off any wood you plan to paint, and never use caulk to cover soft or rotted wood. Cut the rot out and rebuild it with an epoxy product from our <a href="/best-wood-filler/">wood filler guide</a>, then seal the joint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vinyl, fiber cement and composite</h3>

          <p>
            Vinyl grows and shrinks a great deal with temperature, and latex struggles to hold its slick face. A polymer sealant such as OSI Quad is the safer choice, ideally in a color matched to the siding. Fiber cement makers set their own sealant requirements in their installation instructions, usually a high-quality, paintable elastomeric or polymer sealant meeting ASTM C920, so read those before you shop.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brick, stone and stucco</h3>

          <p>
            Masonry is porous, alkaline and often a little dusty. Polymer and tripolymer sealants such as Geocel 2300 hold it much better than latex does, and they cope with the slight dampness masonry tends to keep. Wire-brush the joint edges before you start. When the crack runs through the masonry itself rather than between two materials, a dedicated masonry sealant is the better tool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Glass, aluminum and metal flashing</h3>

          <p>
            Silicone does its best work on nonporous surfaces. For glass-to-frame joints, aluminum window flanges and metal flashing that will stay unpainted, choose 100 percent silicone. If that metal is going to be painted, use a paintable polymer instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="climate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Cold Is Too Cold to Caulk Outside?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most water-based caulks list a minimum application temperature of about 40 degrees Fahrenheit, and that limit covers the surface as well as the air. Polymer and solvent-based sealants usually tolerate colder days, and some are labeled for application near freezing or below. Read the tube for the exact figure, and remember that a north wall at dawn in October can sit several degrees below the forecast high.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold climates: the bead must stay flexible</h3>

          <p>
            Where winters are cold, the cured sealant has to keep its flexibility at low temperatures, because a joint is at its widest once the materials around it have shrunk. That is exactly the moment a stiff caulk tears. Elastomeric latex and polymer sealants hold their stretch in the cold and cheap acrylic does not, which explains why painter's caulk used outdoors tends to fail in winter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hot, sunny climates: UV is the enemy</h3>

          <p>
            In hot, high-sun regions the main threat is ultraviolet light. UV slowly breaks down the binders in latex products and leaves a chalky, cracked surface on south and west walls within a few years. For unpainted joints silicone is the most UV-stable option, and a solid coat of exterior paint over an elastomeric latex sealant shields it from the sun and lengthens its life considerably.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Application temperature is not service temperature</h3>

          <p>
            These are two different limits. The service range describes the weather a cured bead can live through; the application minimum describes the conditions it needs while you lay it and until it skins over. A sealant that shrugs off a hard winter once cured can still fail if it was gunned onto a frosty surface.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TIMING RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Caulk in spring or fall, between mid-morning and mid-afternoon.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The best conditions are dry surfaces, temperatures of roughly 50 to 80 degrees, and no rain for at least the window printed on the tube. A joint sealed at a middling temperature sits at a middling width, so the bead stretches and compresses by similar amounts as the seasons turn.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Skip caulking a dark wall in full afternoon sun in summer. The joint is at its narrowest then, so the bead spends all winter stretched hard, and the hot surface skins the caulk before you can tool it. If the job cannot wait, work the shaded side of the house and move with the shade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="joint-design" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Deep Should Exterior Caulk Be, and When Do You Need Backer Rod?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Aim for a sealant depth of about half the joint width, with a minimum depth of around a quarter inch, and fill anything deeper with closed-cell foam backer rod first. A half-inch-wide joint, for example, wants roughly a quarter inch of sealant. Sealant manufacturers give a typical maximum width of around half to three-quarters of an inch for gun-grade products.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the shape of the bead matters</h3>

          <p>
            A movement rating holds only when the bead has the right profile. The ideal joint is wider than it is deep and bonds to the two sides of the gap but not to its back. That hourglass shape lets the sealant stretch like a rubber band. A bead that is too deep, or stuck to the bottom of the joint, cannot stretch evenly and tears along its edges.
          </p>

