          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What backer rod does</h3>

          <p>
            Backer rod handles three tasks. It sets how deep the bead can go, it gives the tool something to press against, and it stops three-sided adhesion, where sealant grips the bottom of the joint along with the sides. Pick a rod roughly 25 percent wider than the gap so compression holds it in place, press it in with a blunt tool, and avoid puncturing closed-cell rod, which can off-gas and blister the sealant on top.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shallow joints, and gaps too wide to caulk</h3>

          <p>
            Where a joint is too shallow for rod, such as the spot where lap siding meets a corner board, a strip of bond-breaker tape along the bottom does the same job. And once a gap passes about an inch wide, caulk is the wrong repair altogether. Add trim, rebuild the framing or install flashing, then seal the narrower joint that is left.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Do You Caulk Exterior Joints So the Bead Lasts?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Remove the old bead, clean down to bare surfaces, push a properly sized bead into the joint and tool it before it skins. Good sealant applied carelessly fails as quickly as bad sealant, so every one of those steps counts, even though the whole process is short.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Remove the old caulk</h3>

          <p>
            Slice along both edges of the old bead with a sharp knife or a hooked removal tool, pull it out and scrape off what remains. A new bead laid over an old one bonds only to the old material, and that material is already failing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Clean and dry the joint</h3>

          <p>
            Brush out dust and paint flakes, wipe both edges with a rag dampened with isopropyl alcohol and let everything dry. Kill mildew with a diluted bleach solution, then rinse and dry. If silicone was in the joint before, scrub the residue hard with a silicone remover, since almost nothing bonds to a silicone film.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Cut the nozzle and push the bead</h3>

          <p>
            Cut the nozzle at roughly 45 degrees with an opening a little smaller than the joint, and push the bead ahead of the nozzle instead of dragging it behind. Pushing drives sealant into the gap; pulling leaves it sitting on top. Hold steady pressure and move at a pace that fills the joint without heaping it. A dripless gun with a smooth thrust ratio makes this far easier than a bargain-bin gun.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Tool it before it skins</h3>

          <p>
            Tool the bead within a few minutes. A plastic tooling spoon or a wet finger presses sealant against both sides and leaves a slightly concave face that sheds water. For a crisp line on a visible joint, run painter's tape along each side and pull it right after tooling, before the bead skins. Our <a href="/how-to-caulk-exterior-windows/">step-by-step window caulking guide</a> applies the same method to the joint homeowners seal most often.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Work in short sections</h3>

          <p>
            Only run as much bead as you can tool in a few minutes, particularly with silicone and polymers, which skin quickly. In warm weather a three-foot run is plenty. Keep a damp rag handy for latex, or the correct solvent for polymers, and wipe smears at once; polymer sealant that cures on siding is very hard to clean off neatly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cure-paint" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Long Before Exterior Caulk Can Get Rained On or Painted?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Labels list rain-ready times from under an hour for some silicones to several hours for latex, and latex caulks typically accept paint within an hour or two to a day. Polymers often need longer before paint, and silicone never takes it. Caulk cures from the outside in: the skin forms within minutes to an hour, at which point light rain or dust will not mark it, but the core stays soft much longer. A normal-sized latex bead needs several days for a full cure, and a thick polymer or silicone bead can need a week or more. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-caulk-take-to-dry/">how long caulk takes to dry</a> sorts the timing by chemistry and conditions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The rain window</h3>

          <p>
            Rain is the deadline that matters. Water-based caulk that gets soaked before it skins can wash out of the joint or cure with a weak, chalky surface. Check the forecast for the label's rain-ready window plus a margin, and count dew as moisture on cool evenings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When you can paint</h3>

          <p>
            Paint timing is where chemistry matters again. Latex and elastomeric latex caulks usually take paint within an hour or two up to a day, depending on the product and conditions. Polymer sealants often need longer, and some makers recommend waiting until the bead has firmed up. Silicone cannot be painted at all. Paint applied too early can crack as the bead underneath keeps moving and shrinking.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which paint to put over caulk</h3>

          <p>
            Topcoat sealant with a quality exterior acrylic paint rather than an oil-based one, which is stiffer and tends to crack over a flexible bead. Paint shields latex sealant from UV and is one of the cheapest ways to add years to a caulk job. Carrying the paint a few millimeters past each edge of the joint also seals the bond line, which is exactly where water tries to sneak in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Many Tubes of Caulk Do You Need, and What Does It Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plan on about one 10.1 oz tube per window and rarely more than 10 to 20 tubes for a whole house. At the time of writing, basic acrylic caulk costs roughly $3 to $6 per tube, elastomeric latex roughly $6 to $12, and polymer, silicone and tripolymer sealants roughly $8 to $15, so the gap between the cheapest and the best for a whole house is usually under $100. Sealant is cheap next to the damage it prevents, which makes it a poor place to save a few dollars a tube.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How far does one tube go?</h3>

          <p>
            Coverage comes down almost entirely to bead size. A standard 10.1 oz cartridge holds a little over 18 cubic inches of sealant. That fills about 24 linear feet of a square quarter-inch joint and stretches toward 90 feet at an eighth of an inch. A triangular fillet bead on an outside corner uses less material per foot than a full-depth joint, which is why the coverage printed on tube labels often runs higher than the math for a filled gap.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
