                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wide trim joints with backer rod</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Estimating a whole house</h3>

          <p>
            Measure the perimeter of every window and door, add corner boards, butt joints and penetrations, and divide the total by the coverage for your usual bead. Then add 20 percent for waste, tooling and the tube that always cures in its nozzle overnight. A standard double-hung window perimeter runs about 18 to 20 feet, which is why one tube per window is a realistic planning figure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where to spend and where to save</h3>

          <p>
            Put premium sealant on the joints that face weather and move, and keep budget acrylic for small, sheltered, painted gaps. Skip the bulk 28 oz cartridges unless the job is large and continuous: an opened cartridge cures within days, and the waste cancels out the savings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. How Long Does Exterior Caulk Last, and When Should You Recaulk?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plain acrylic caulk on a sunny wall may last only a few years, a good elastomeric latex under paint commonly lasts much longer, and silicone and polymer sealants are often rated for decades on the label. Those durability claims assume correct joint design and surface prep, so a bead laid over old caulk or without backer rod will fall short of them. Service life also varies with exposure, which is why the sunny south wall usually needs attention first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Inspect twice a year</h3>

          <p>
            Even the best exterior sealant needs upkeep. Walk around the house each spring and fall and look at window and door perimeters, trim joints, pipe and wire penetrations, and every joint where two different materials meet. The aim is to find failing beads before water finds a path into the wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Four signs it is time to recaulk</h3>

          <p>
            Replace the bead when it has pulled away from one side of the joint, cracked all the way through, turned hard and brittle, or shrunk so far that daylight shows through the joint. Fine crazing on a painted bead is often only cosmetic, so press it with a fingernail. If the sealant underneath is still rubbery and holding on both sides, a fresh coat of paint may be all it needs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Caulk is one part of the water plan</h3>

          <p>
            Sealant is one piece of a larger system for managing water. Keep gutters flowing so water does not sheet down the siding and overload the joints, which is where a set of <a href="/best-gutter-guards/">gutter guards</a> can help, and keep wood protected with a good finish such as the options in our <a href="/best-exterior-wood-sealer/">exterior wood sealer guide</a>. Caulk keeps water out of the gaps; the rest of the building envelope should keep most of it off the walls in the first place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes With Exterior Caulk
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using painter's caulk on siding joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Inexpensive acrylic caulk labeled only to ASTM C834 has very little stretch. Across butt joints, corner boards and sunny walls it cracks within a season or two, because the joint moves further than the bead can follow. On any joint that works, use an elastomeric latex or a polymer sealant that carries an ASTM C920 movement class.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking over the old bead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new bead laid on top of a failing one bonds only to the old material, and the old material is already letting go. Within months both layers peel away together. Cut the old sealant out completely, scrape off the residue and clean the edges, then lay the new bead against bare, sound surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling deep gaps with sealant alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bead deeper than it is wide cannot stretch evenly, shrinks as it cures and swallows several tubes, and it tends to bond to the back of the joint as well. Press in closed-cell backer rod about a quarter larger than the gap so the sealant ends up roughly half as deep as the joint is wide.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting silicone where paint is planned</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint beads up and peels on silicone, and its residue keeps contaminating the joint long after the bead itself has been cut out. If a joint will ever be painted, start with a paintable elastomeric or polymer sealant. Reserve silicone for glass, metal and the joints you are certain will stay unpainted.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing weep holes and drainage gaps</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some gaps are supposed to stay open. Window sill weep slots, the bottom edge of window nailing flanges in some installations, brick weep holes and the bottom of lap siding courses all let trapped water drain away. Caulk them shut and a minor leak turns into rot hidden inside the wall.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which caulk should most homeowners buy for outdoor joints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On most painted houses, an elastomeric latex sealant such as DAP Dynaflex 230 is the best all-around choice. It cleans up with water, takes paint, grips the usual siding and trim materials and handles far more joint movement than ordinary acrylic caulk. Move to a polymer for vinyl and masonry, or to silicone for glass and metal that will never be painted.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is silicone or latex better for exterior joints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That turns on whether the joint will be painted. Silicone is more waterproof and more UV-stable, but paint will not bond to it and it holds poorly on porous wood and masonry. Elastomeric latex takes paint, is easy to work and flexes enough for most siding and trim. Choose latex for painted wood, and silicone for glass, aluminum and unpainted metal.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What service life can I expect from an outdoor sealant?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plain acrylic caulk on a sunny wall can fail inside a few years. A quality elastomeric latex protected by paint commonly lasts much longer, and many silicone and polymer sealants carry multi-decade durability claims on the label. Those claims assume sound joint design, backer rod where it is needed and a bead laid on clean, bare surfaces, so prep matters as much as the tube you pick.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I caulk exterior joints in cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most water-based caulks list a minimum application temperature of about 40 degrees Fahrenheit, for the surface as well as the air, and they need to stay above freezing until they skin over. Some polymer and solvent-based sealants are labeled for colder conditions. Read the tube, check the surface temperature on shaded walls, and hold off when frost or dew may arrive before the bead skins.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What caulk works on vinyl siding?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Vinyl moves a lot with temperature and has a slick face, so a polymer sealant such as OSI Quad is the usual choice. It holds vinyl well, stays flexible and comes in colors matched to common siding shades. Leave the overlaps where vinyl panels lock together uncaulked; they are built to slide and to drain, and sealing them makes the siding buckle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use caulk on brick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Where brick meets wood trim, windows or doors, use a polymer or tripolymer sealant rather than acrylic. Products such as Geocel 2300 hold porous, slightly damp masonry well and cope with the movement between unlike materials. Wire-brush loose material off the edges first. Never seal brick weep holes, and repair cracked mortar joints with mortar, not caulk.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need backer rod for every joint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Joints a quarter inch wide or narrower, and shallow joints, usually need no rod. Any joint deeper than about half its width should get closed-cell foam backer rod so the sealant ends up roughly half as deep as it is wide. Where rod will not fit, bond-breaker tape along the bottom of the joint prevents three-sided adhesion.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

