        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            DAP Dynaflex 230 is the sensible default for most painted houses: easy to apply, paintable and flexible enough for real exterior joints. Switch to Sashco Big Stretch where joints keep cracking, OSI Quad for vinyl and fiber cement, Geocel 2300 for metal and masonry, and GE Advanced Silicone 2 for glass and unpainted metal.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The tube is only half of the result. Cut out the old bead, clean down to bare surfaces, size the joint with backer rod, push the bead and tool it, and paint once the label says you can. Follow those steps and a mid-priced sealant will outlast a premium tube laid over old caulk.
          </p>
